  "answer_text": "If you are using a glass for a week without washing it\u2014even if you only refill it with filtered water from the fridge\u2014the whitish film that develops is almost certainly caused by one of two things (or a combination of both): **mineral buildup (limescale)** or a **microbial biofilm**. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what these are, why they happen even with filtered water, and how to tell them apart.\n\n---\n\n### Suspect #1: Mineral Buildup (Limescale)\nEven though your water is filtered, most standard refrigerator filters (which typically use activated carbon) are designed to improve taste and remove contaminants like chlorine, lead, and volatile organic compounds (VOCs). **They do not remove dissolved minerals like calcium and magnesium.**\n\n* **How it forms:** Every time you take a sip or empty the glass, a thin layer of water is left clinging to the inside of the glass. As that water evaporates into the air, it leaves behind those dissolved minerals (mostly calcium carbonate). Over the course of a week, these micro-layers of minerals stack up, leaving a visible, dry, white, or chalky film.\n* **Texture:** It will feel slightly rough, dry, or chalky to the touch.\n\n### Suspect #2: Biofilm (Bacterial/Yeast Colony)\nOur mouths are naturally home to billions of harmless bacteria, yeasts, and food enzymes. \n\n* **How it forms:** Every time you take a drink, a small amount of saliva, lip oils, and skin cells transfer to the rim of the glass and wash back into the water. Over a week of sitting at room temperature, these microbes feed on the trace organic matter from your mouth. They multiply and anchor themselves to the glass, secreting a slimy, protective matrix known as a **biofilm** (similar to the plaque that forms on your teeth or the slick film in a pet's water bowl). \n* **Texture:** It will feel slick, slippery, or slightly slimy when wet. \n\n---\n\n### How to Tell Them Apart: The \"Vinegar Test\"\nYou can easily diagnose exactly what the film is with a simple test using white vinegar:\n\n1. **If it dissolves instantly with vinegar:** It is **mineral buildup**. Vinegar is highly acidic and will immediately break down and dissolve calcium carbonate. \n2. **If it requires soap and scrubbing to remove:** It is a **biofilm**. Biofilms are water-resistant and hold onto the glass tightly; they require the physical friction of a sponge and the lipid-breaking power of dish soap to be washed away.\n\n### Is it safe?\n* **Minerals** are entirely harmless to digest. \n* **Biofilms** are usually made of your own mouth bacteria, meaning they are unlikely to make you sick under normal circumstances. However, leaving a glass unwashed for a week allows ambient mold, airborne yeast, or dust to settle in. If you happen to be getting sick, a week-old unwashed glass can also harbor viruses or bacteria that could reinfect you or make others sick.\n\n### How to prevent it\nTo keep your glass sparkling clean and hygienic, try to **wash it with warm, soapy water every 1 to 2 days**. If hard water minerals are your main issue, giving the glass a quick swirl with vinegar once a week will keep the cloudy film from returning.",
